# Parabolic Shapiro dimension comparison with central-fixed coinduction

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.parabolicH1_finrank_le_centralCoinduced`
(`f7d52bf4-45cb-48e0-bad8-d2c3eeb2269f`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `2dcc4327-2f83-47e5-9ae2-6463e9f450d1`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$, $n\ge0$, $H=\Gamma_1(N)$, $G=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, and
$V=\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathbb C^2$ with the MTT substitution action. Let
$W=\operatorname{Coind}_H^G V$ and let $W^+$ be the subrepresentation fixed by $-I$. Then
$$\dim_{\mathbb C}H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(H,V)\le \dim_{\mathbb C}H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(G,W^+).$$ In both
spaces, parabolic means principal upon restriction to each rational-cusp stabilizer. This comparison
is the parabolic Shapiro dimension inequality after removing the nontrivial central summand. It
applies in every degree, with no parity assumption, and permits subsequent full-group estimates on a
representation with trivial central action.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Parabolic Shapiro comparison after central averaging

Put $H=\Gamma_1(N)$, $G=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$,
$V=\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathbb C^2$, and $W=\operatorname{Coind}_H^G V$.
Write $W^+$ for the fixed space of the central element $z=-I$.
We prove

$$\dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(H,V)\le
  \dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(G,W^+).$$

First, evaluation at the identity sends a $W$-valued cocycle $c$ to the
$V$-valued cocycle $h\mapsto c(h)(1)$. Coinduction's equivariance identity
makes this a linear cocycle map. Evaluation sends a principal witness $P$
to $P(1)$, both for ordinary coboundaries and for each rational-cusp restriction.
It therefore descends to parabolic cohomology. It is surjective by the already
accepted parabolic Shapiro lifting theorem
`96bbed5a-210d-4d60-8934-d83a912e2875` (proof
`aef03d31-6fe8-4eaa-bbfb-2f0d979ac951`). This avoids choosing a new linear lift.

Next consider any complex $G$-representation $A$. The averaging projection
$p(v)=\frac12(v+\rho(z)v)$ takes values in $A^{z=1}$ and commutes with every
group element. If $c$ is a cocycle, its equation applied to $zg=gz$ gives

$$p(c(g))=c(g)+\rho(g)Q-Q,\qquad Q=\tfrac12c(z).$$

Thus averaging changes a cocycle only by a principal cocycle and preserves its
parabolic restrictions. If the averaged cocycle is principal with witness $P$
in the fixed space, the original is principal with witness $P-Q$ in $A$.
Conversely, a principal witness averages to a fixed-space witness. This proves
that averaging induces an injective map from parabolic cohomology with
coefficients in $A$ to that with coefficients in $A^{z=1}$.

All dimension comparisons are legitimate: coinduction is finite-dimensional
for a finite-index subgroup, using the already-Proved platform theorem
`e8c7e524-83e3-5eb1-8320-90e917319796`. A cocycle on a finitely generated group
is determined by its values on generators; for $G$, use $S,T$. Hence the relevant
cocycle spaces, subspaces, and quotients are finite-dimensional. Combining the
surjective Shapiro counit with the injective central projection gives the claim.

The proof applies to all $N>0$ and all $n\ge0$, with no parity hypothesis. It does
not assume that $-I$ acts trivially on the whole coinduced module. It introduces
no numerical cohomology bound, cusp-form estimate, custom axiom, sorry,
native_decide, or resource-limit override. Only the two already-Proved inputs
above are used, together with the published coefficient definitions and Mathlib.

